      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The missing or incorrect card verification is a common issue. You see, Stripe's API is quite particular about the card verification process, and if it's not done correctly, it can lead to errors like the 402 error. It's a bit like trying to sneak past a goblin guard without being detected - you need to make sure you're following the right protocol.\n\nTo inspect the JSON payload, you can use a tool like Postman or cURL to send a request to the Stripe API and view the response. You can also use the Stripe API's built-in debugging tools, such as the Stripe Dashboard's \"Test\" tab, to see the response and any errors that may occur.\n\nTo inspect the JSON payload, you can use the `curl` command or a tool like Postman to send a request to the Stripe API. Here's an example of how you can use `curl` to send a request and view the response:\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \\\n  https://api.stripe.com/v1/charges \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"amount\": 1000, \"currency\": \"usd\", \"source\": \"card_123456789\", \"description\":",
